  "query_preview": "..Mid-level clouds are slowly dissipating across the\nregion with just scattered clouds west of Tucson. Expect mostly\ndry conditions this week with the exception of the White\nMountains Wednesday and possibly Thursday afternoon as a weak \nshortwave moves through. NBM has slight PoPs (15 percent) both \ndays. A couple HREF members show an isolated storm or two \nforming Wednesday afternoon. Otherwise temperatures will be 4-8 \ndegrees above normal through the end of the work week with \nwestern Pima County likely seeing triple digit highs thanks to \nhigh pressure overhead. Also, starting Thursday into Saturday \nwinds turn easterly and breezy again. Right now speeds don't \nlook quite as strong as this past weekend, likely topping out \n15-20 mph with gusts to 35 mph. The strongest winds will be in \nthe Gila River Valley. \n\nThis weekend ensembles have consistently been showing a trough\nsetting up over the west coast bringing rain chances, cooler\ntemperatures, and breezy southwest winds. There is fairly high\nconfidence in the existence of the trough, the main uncertainty\nis its strength and amplitude. To complicate matters more it \nwill likely interact with Hurricane Rachel, which is currently a\nfew hundred miles off the west coast of Baja. As the trough \ndigs south it seems increasingly likely that Rachel will turn \nand head eastward. There is lots of uncertainty in where exactly\nit could end up as it travels east. Current ensembles show \ntracks anywhere from Oxnard down to northern Baja California \nSur. Even with this uncertainty there is growing confidence in a\nwetter forecast starting Sunday into the first part of next \nweek. Current NBM PoPs are 20-50 percent for most of southeast \nArizona late Saturday night into next Tuesday.",
